From 0d3187c5e5788374720e997be0974e2e4b0c47c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jesse Casco <jesse.casco@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 22:07:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: glymur: enable SCMI polling mode

The CPUCP on glymur services the SCMI Performance protocol correctly but
never raises the completion interrupt for a reply. Every transaction
therefore runs to the 30 ms rx timeout, protocol probe fails, and the
platform comes up with no cpufreq at all: the CPUs are left parked at
whatever OPP the bootloader chose.

Set arm,no-completion-irq on the SCMI node so the transport falls back to
polling for completion, which the firmware does support.

Tested on an ASUS Zenbook A16 (UX3607OA, X2E94100). Without the property
there are no cpufreq policies; with it all three policies register and
scale, and the SCMI core reports "Enabled polling mode TX channel".

Assisted-by: Anthropic:Claude-Opus-5
Signed-off-by: Jesse Casco <jesse.casco@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi
index f04058d2e089..38f9d39fb612 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi
@@ -411,6 +411,7 @@ scmi {
 			mboxes = <&pdp0_mbox 0>, <&pdp0_mbox 1>;
 			mbox-names = "tx", "rx";
 			shmem = <&cpu_scp_lpri1>, <&cpu_scp_lpri0>;
+			arm,no-completion-irq;
 
 			#address-cells = <1>;
 			#size-cells = <0>;
